course: dictionary
lesson: 12656
title: Losing your good name: disrepute
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disrepute means having a bad name, so people think badly of someone or something. «The coach lied about the score, and the whole club fell into disrepute.» After the lie, people no longer respect the club.
explain: Disrepute is a formal word, so you will meet it most in careful writing, news and rules. Look for two phrases: «bring something into disrepute», which means make it look bad, and «fall into disrepute», which means lose respect. Watch for the words bring and fall.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «He cheated in the test, which brought the school into disrepute.» Cheating is wrong, so the school now looks bad. Try your meaning in the sentence: "which made the school look bad" fits, so disrepute is about how others see you.
fact: Disrepute is a noun, and it is uncountable: you cannot say "a disrepute" or "two disreputes".
fact: Disrepute means having a bad name, or losing the respect people used to have.
fact: «Bring something into disrepute» means make it look bad.
fact: «Fall into disrepute» means lose respect, and it can happen to a person, a group or an activity.
fact: Disrepute is a formal word, so it is used more in books, news and school or sports rules than in everyday talk.
exercise: «The lies he told brought the school into disrepute.» What does brought into disrepute mean here? | Made the school very happy | *Made the school look bad | Gave the school a new name | Made the school bigger
exercise: Which phrase means nearly the same as disrepute? | Fast running | Heavy rain | *Loss of respect | Great fame
exercise: «The old house had fallen into disrepute.» What does this suggest? | It had become a famous palace | People came to visit it every day | *People no longer respected it | It was painted bright colours
test: «After the team cheated, the club fell into disrepute.» What word is disrepute? | An adverb, a word for how something is done | An adjective, a describing word | A verb, an action word | *A noun, a name for a state of being
test: Which phrase means to make something look bad? | Rise to fame | *Bring into disrepute | Fall into popularity | Come into fashion
exam: «After the coach was caught cheating, the whole team fell into disrepute.» What does disrepute mean here? | Being given a prize | *Losing the respect people had for them | Winning every game in the league | Being very tired after a match
